        "orders": [
          {
            "run_id": "20260907T075027Z-claude-you-are-collating-the-results-of",
            "strongest": "cand-grok",
            "reason": "Both diffs are minimal and leave greet untouched, but composer's slug() has a correctness bug: it only pops a trailing hyphen after the loop and never guards against a leading one, so inputs beginning with a non-alnum character (e.g. \\\"-abc\\\", \\\"  Hello World!!\\\", \\\"!!!abc!!!\\\") produce a leading hyphen \u2014 violating the explicit \\\"no leading or trailing hyphen\\\" spec. I verified this by running both implementations directly. Grok's version uses a pending-hyphen flag gated on `out` being non-empty, which correctly suppresses hyphens at both ends and passed all tested cases (leading, trailing, runs of separators, all-separator input).",
            "invalid": null,
            "scores": {
              "cand-composer": 3,
              "cand-grok": 9
            }
          },
          {
            "run_id": "20260907T075027Z-claude-you-are-collating-the-results-of-2",
            "strongest": "cand-grok",
            "reason": "Composer's implementation has a bug: it only strips a trailing hyphen, not a leading one. For input starting with a non-alnum character (e.g. \\\"-abc\\\" or \\\" abc\\\"), it emits a leading hyphen immediately since the initial `hyphen` flag is False, violating the 'no leading hyphen' requirement. Grok's version guards leading hyphens correctly via `elif out:` (only queues a hyphen once at least one character has been emitted) and also strips a trailing pending hyphen naturally by never flushing it. Both otherwise correctly lowercase and collapse runs of non-ASCII-alnum characters and leave `greet` untouched, but grok satisfies the spec exactly while composer does not.",
            "invalid": null,
            "scores": {
              "cand-grok": 9,
